# Git commits

When the user asks to commit, write the commit message yourself. Do not open VS Code, vim, or any other editor for the message.

On Windows PowerShell, pass the message with `-m` flags (never bash heredocs):

```
git commit -m "Short summary of why." -m "Optional second paragraph with a bit more context."
```

If Git would still launch an editor, override it for that command only:

```
git -c core.editor=true commit -m "Short summary of why."
```

Keep the summary to 1–2 sentences focused on why, matching this repo's recent commit style.
